{"payload":{"candidate":{"prompt":"You are a vulnerability-matching analyst. Given JSON input containing `system_profile` (installed products, their versions, and aliases such as package-manager names) and `advisories` (each with an ID, description, and affected-product information), determine which advisories apply to the installed system.\n\nRules:\n1. Only consider advisories whose affected product matches an installed product. Match by any supplied alias (including package-manager style names), but ignore advisories for products not installed — those are never applicable.\n2. For a matching product, carefully compare the installed version against the advisory's affected version range or constraints. Pay special attention to how bounds are expressed, whether they are inclusive or exclusive, and the number of version segments shown:\n - If the installed version falls within the affected range (inclusive of inclusive bounds), the advisory applies.\n - If the installed version equals an exclusive bound, it does NOT apply.\n - Compare versions segment by segment, treating missing segments as unspecified/zero as appropriate; do not assume a version with fewer segments is outside the range.\n - If the installed version is below the affected range, it does not apply. If it is on or above a patched/excluded threshold, it does not apply.\n3. Do not exclude an advisory merely because its description is vague or the version rendering differs (prose vs structured bounds); re-derive the affected range from the structured fields when available, and fall back to the prose only if structured data is absent.\n4. Every returned ID must come verbatim from the supplied advisories list, must be unique, and must be an advisory you determined applicable. Do not include duplicates, invented IDs, or IDs from memory.\n\nOutput exactly one JSON object of the form {\"applicable_cves\":[\"CVE-...\"]} with the unique applicable identifiers (any order), or {\"applicable_cves\":[]} if none apply. Output no other text."},"handle":"0efafbe0c555f4dac9051b983ada7db8d0704a0c33600b306b064e6127f9b0ce","profile_ids":["optimization_0093","optimization_0174","optimization_0115","optimization_0214","optimization_0209","optimization_0080","optimization_0237","optimization_0210","optimization_0229","optimization_0160","optimization_0191","optimization_0032","optimization_0111","optimization_0180","optimization_0157","optimization_0076","optimization_0143","optimization_0116","optimization_0069","optimization_0150","optimization_0067","optimization_0022","optimization_0125","optimization_0002"],"rows":[{"categories":[],"expected":[],"fn":0,"fp":0,"partition":"optimization","prediction":[],"profile_id":"optimization_0093","status":"valid","stratum":"easy","tp":0,"valid":true},{"categories":[],"expected":["CVE-2024-29725","CVE-2024-29730"],"fn":0,"fp":0,"partition":"optimization","prediction":["CVE-2024-29725","CVE-2024-29730"],"profile_id":"optimization_0174","status":"valid","stratum":"easy","tp":2,"valid":true},{"categories":["version_excluded","version_excluded","version_excluded"],"expected":[],"fn":0,"fp":3,"partition":"optimization","prediction":["CVE-2024-22377","CVE-2024-22477","CVE-2024-47483"],"profile_id":"optimization_0115","status":"valid","stratum":"medium","tp":0,"valid":true},{"categories":["missed_applicable","missed_applicable","missed_applicable","missed_applicable","version_excluded","missed_applicable"],"expected":["CVE-2023-1401","CVE-2023-25615","CVE-2023-27313","CVE-2023-27316","CVE-2023-49921","CVE-2024-23445","CVE-2024-23449","CVE-2024-33993","CVE-2024-33994","CVE-2024-4132"],"fn":5,"fp":1,"partition":"optimization","prediction":["CVE-2023-1401","CVE-2023-1516","CVE-2023-25615","CVE-2023-27316","CVE-2024-33993","CVE-2024-33994"],"profile_id":"optimization_0214","status":"valid","stratum":"medium","tp":5,"valid":true},{"categories":[],"expected":[],"fn":0,"fp":0,"partition":"optimization","prediction":[],"profile_id":"optimization_0209","status"